    $astroMars_master$> Mars is where the argument of Unit II completes itself. In L7 I promised a causal chain that would convert the isolated fact "Mars has no magnetic field" into a sequence running from a planet's *size* to its *habitability* — and this lecture runs it to the end. **Mars is small: a surface-to-volume ratio 1.88 times Earth's, so it cooled about twice as fast.** Its core stopped convecting, its dynamo died — and we can date that death to before **4.1 billion years ago**, because Mars Global Surveyor found magnetized ancient crust everywhere *except* around the great impact basins Hellas and Argyre, which demagnetized on impact and never re-magnetized. After that the solar wind stripped the atmosphere — MAVEN watches it happening in real time — until surface pressure fell below a decisive threshold. Mars's pressure today is **6.36 millibars**; water's triple point is **6.11**. Mars sits, almost exactly, at the pressure below which liquid water cannot exist at any temperature whatsoever: rivers are thermodynamically impossible. Yet Mars is covered in dried riverbeds, deltas, and clays that only form in standing water. **This planet had rivers and lakes for something like a billion years — a span that overlaps precisely the period when life was establishing itself on Earth.** Then it lost its air, fell below the triple point, and froze. We close Unit II with the question that has sent more spacecraft to Mars than anywhere else: in that billion years of warmth and water, **did anything live?**

## The storm that hid a world

In L7 we began with **Mariner 4**, whose 1965 flyby returned twenty-one photographs of a cratered, lunar-looking Mars and left the impression — hardened over the flybys that followed — that Mars was essentially a bigger, redder Moon: dead and dull. That impression was an artefact of where the flybys happened to look. In November 1971 **Mariner 9** arrived and became the **first spacecraft to orbit another planet** — and found nothing at all. Mars was engulfed in a **planet-encircling dust storm**, its surface a featureless orange haze. The most sophisticated planetary spacecraft yet built had crossed 200 million kilometres to photograph a blank. So the team waited, for weeks.

As the dust settled, the first features to emerge were four dark spots poking *up through the haze* — not surface markings but the **summits of enormous volcanoes**, standing so tall their peaks rose above a dust layer covering an entire planet. One, Olympus Mons, is **21.9 km high**. And as the air cleared, Mariner 9 revealed a canyon system four thousand kilometres long, layered polar deposits recording cycles of climate, and then the discovery that changed everything: **dendritic valley networks** — branching, tributary-fed channels with exactly the geometry that flowing water carves and nothing else plausibly does. Mariner 9 transformed Mars for the second time in six years, from dead rock into a geologically dramatic world with a **watery past**. Every mission since has pursued its implication — because a planet with dried riverbeds poses a question a merely dead planet does not. Not "what is Mars like?" but **"what happened here?"**

{{image: Valles Marineris | Valles Marineris, the canyon system Mariner 9 revealed once the great dust storm of 1971 cleared — some 4,000 km long, a fifth of the way around the planet, up to 200 km wide and 7 km deep. It is not a river canyon but a tectonic rift, torn open when the enormous Tharsis volcanic bulge loaded and stretched the crust — a structure Earth's ever-moving plates never let accumulate. Mariner 9 was the first spacecraft to orbit another planet, and by waiting and mapping the whole surface it overturned the "bigger, redder Moon" picture the flybys had left, exposing volcanoes, layered polar deposits, and the dendritic valley networks that proved Mars once ran with water.}}

## A world just below the threshold

Before reconstructing the past, fix the present, because one number does most of the work. Mars has a radius of 3,390 km (surface gravity 0.38 g, escape velocity 5.03 km/s), a mean temperature of 210 K, and a surface pressure of just **6.36 millibars — about 1/159 of Earth's**, closer to a laboratory vacuum than to air. Its atmosphere is 95% CO₂, the same gas that cooks Venus, yet its greenhouse effect is only about **+6 K** — because there is so little of it. *A thin atmosphere of a greenhouse gas is still a thin atmosphere*, a useful corrective to the intuition that composition alone sets climate.

Now the decisive physics. Water's **triple point** — the unique pressure and temperature where solid, liquid, and vapour coexist — is at **6.11 mbar** and 273 K, and below that pressure **liquid water cannot exist at any temperature whatsoever**: heat ice and it sublimates straight to vapour, as dry ice does in open air. Mars's mean pressure is 6.36 mbar. **It sits, to within four percent, exactly at the threshold below which liquid water is forbidden.**

```triplepoint
```

The consequence is that across most of Mars, at most times, liquid water is impossible regardless of temperature. In the deepest basins — Hellas is 7 km down, where pressure reaches ~12 mbar — liquid is *permitted* by pressure, but it is far too cold there to occur. The two conditions are never comfortably met at once. **This is why Mars is a frozen desert despite holding a great deal of water: the water is there; the atmosphere is not.**

```example
mars-triple-point
```

And it sharpens Mariner 9's question. If liquid water is impossible now, and the valley networks prove it flowed then, **the atmosphere must once have been far thicker**. The task is to show that it was, and to explain where it went.

## The evidence for ancient water

The case that Mars once had abundant surface water is now overwhelming, and it comes from several independent classes of evidence. **Landforms:** the *valley networks*, dated by crater counting (calibrated by lunar samples, L11) to ~3.7 Gyr, whose tributary geometry records distributed *precipitation and runoff*; enormous *outflow channels* carved by catastrophic floods; and the *delta* in Jezero crater, a fan of sediment where an inflowing river met a **standing lake**. **Minerals**, which are harder to explain away because certain ones form only in water: *clay minerals* (phyllosilicates) require prolonged rock–water interaction at neutral pH — sustained wet conditions, not a brief flood; *hematite spherules* (Opportunity's "blueberries") are groundwater concretions; and *sulphates like jarosite* require acidic water, recording a drift from early neutral to later acidic conditions as the planet dried. **Sediments:** Curiosity photographed *conglomerates* of cemented, rounded pebbles whose size and shape imply a stream flowing at **hip depth and walking pace** — not merely "water was here" but *a stream of this depth and speed ran here*.

Taken together: Mars had rain or snowmelt, rivers, lakes, deltas, groundwater, and probably seas, for a period of order a billion years, roughly **4.1 to 3.0 billion years ago**. That window matters enormously, because **life was established on Earth by 3.5 Gyr and possibly 3.8** — so Martian surface water overlapped the era when life arose here. Then the water stopped: the valley networks cease, the mineralogy shifts to dry and oxidised, and the surface has been static since. **Something turned Mars off.**

## From size to death

Here is the heart of the lecture, and the completion of L7's promise: the full sequence, with evidence at each link. Mars is **small** — and *why* is itself Unit I's answer, a feeding zone truncated by Jupiter's excursion (the Mars problem, L5–L6) — giving A/V **1.88× Earth's**, so it **cooled about twice as fast**. Rapid cooling starved the core of the heat flow a dynamo needs; convection ceased and the **dynamo died**. And we can date that death, by a genuinely elegant argument: Mars Global Surveyor found intensely magnetized ancient crust, but *none* around the Hellas and Argyre basins. Large impacts heat rock past its Curie point and erase its magnetism — so an unmagnetized basin means the field was already gone when it cooled, since a live field would have re-magnetized it. Those basins date to **4.1 Gyr**, so the dynamo died within the first ~500 Myr. (InSight later found the core is still *liquid* — so it did not freeze; it stopped *convecting*, exactly the requirement L7 flagged.)

```causalchain
```

With no shield, the **solar wind** struck the upper atmosphere directly, and Mars's low escape velocity made escape easy — small size penalised Mars twice, killing the shield *and* lowering the barrier. This is not theory alone: **MAVEN has measured the escape directly since 2014**, watching it spike tenfold during solar storms. The isotopes give the total — argon enriched in its heavy isotope implies **two-thirds** has escaped (and argon is inert, so escape is the only route), while D/H at 5–6× Earth's shows much of the water is gone too. As the air thinned, pressure crossed **6.11 mbar**, liquid water became impossible, and the planet froze into the desert we see. **Eight links from a planet's radius to its habitability**, with independent evidence at nearly every one.

```example
av-cooling
```

The story is cleaner in outline than in detail, and two caveats are honest. Producing a genuinely *warm* early Mars is hard in climate models — the young Sun was fainter (worse at 1.52 AU), and even thick CO₂ struggles, partly because it condenses out; some favour an "icy highlands" Mars that was mostly frozen with intermittent thaws. And the **carbon budget does not fully balance**: escape accounts for much of the lost CO₂, but the expected carbonate rock is found in smaller quantities than a thick early atmosphere predicts.

```example
dynamo-shutdown
```

## What the absence of tectonics built

Mars's landforms are shaped as much by what it *lacks* as by what it has. **Olympus Mons is 21.9 km high and 600 km across** — two and a half times taller and five times wider than Earth's Mauna Loa, the largest volcano in the solar system. Why? *Because Mars has no plate tectonics.* On Earth a mantle plume feeds a volcano, but the overlying plate is moving, so the volcano is carried off the hotspot and dies while a new one grows — producing a *chain*, precisely the Hawaiian–Emperor seamounts that record 80 Myr of Pacific-plate motion. On Mars the lithosphere is a **stagnant lid**: it does not move, so a hotspot feeds the same point for hundreds of millions of years and the volcano simply keeps growing (helped by low gravity, which lets a taller edifice stand). **Olympus Mons is a monument to the absence of plate tectonics** — same process as Hawaii, opposite tectonic regime, radically different landform.

{{image: Olympus Mons | Olympus Mons, the largest volcano in the solar system — 21.9 km high and some 600 km across, dwarfing Earth's Mauna Loa. Its size is a direct consequence of what Mars lacks: with no plate tectonics, the stagnant lithosphere never moves, so a single hotspot has poured lava onto the same point for hundreds of millions of years instead of being dragged away. On Earth the moving Pacific plate turns the same process into the Hawaiian–Emperor chain of modest volcanoes, each carried off its plume in turn. Same underlying process, opposite tectonic regime — and a landform Earth can never build. Low Martian gravity helps too, letting a taller edifice support its own weight.}}

The same logic explains **Valles Marineris**, not a river canyon but a tectonic rift torn by the loading of the vast Tharsis bulge — a volcanic province that could only accumulate because no moving plates carried it away. On Mars, the absence of tectonics writes itself across the whole planet.

## Did anything live there?

We close Unit II with the question that drives Mars exploration, and the case is chronological, not romantic: Mars had liquid water, energy, and the necessary elements for about a billion years, overlapping the interval when life arose on Earth. If the origin of life is a reasonably probable outcome given water and time — which is exactly what we do not know — then Mars had both. And Mars is uniquely valuable because it **preserves its ancient surface**: Earth's plate tectonics destroyed nearly all rock from the relevant era, but geologically-dead Mars still has 3.7-billion-year-old lakebeds sitting exposed. **The record we cannot read on Earth may be readable on Mars.**

{{image: Jezero (crater) | Jezero crater, where Perseverance has been exploring since 2021 — the fan of sediment at the lower left is an ancient river delta, built where a Martian river once emptied into a standing lake around 3.7 billion years ago. Deltas are among the best settings anywhere for preserving biosignatures, which is why this was chosen: the rover is caching sealed samples for possible return to Earth. That is the argument of L11 applied to the biggest question in the field — a returned sample is a permanent capability, re-analysable as techniques improve, and Mars Sample Return is realistically the only way to settle whether anything ever lived here.}}

In 1976 the two **Viking** landers ran the only dedicated life-detection experiments ever performed on another planet, and the results remain a lesson in evidence. The *Labeled Release* experiment gave a **positive** signal — released gas from labelled nutrients, with a heat-sterilised control behaving exactly as a biological reading predicts. But the gas chromatograph–mass spectrometer found **no organic molecules at all**, and for most of the community that was decisive: metabolism without organic chemistry is not credible, so Viking was recorded as a null. Then, in 2008, **Phoenix discovered perchlorates** in Martian soil — powerful oxidisers that, when heated as the GCMS heated its samples, *destroy organic molecules*. So Viking may have incinerated the very organics it sought, while perchlorate chemistry can mimic the Labeled Release signal without biology. Curiosity has since confirmed organics *are* present (2014, 2018). The most likely reading remains **chemistry, not life** — but the episode is a genuine cautionary tale: a negative result was trusted, and the negative turned out to be an instrument destroying its own target. **Remote life detection is far harder than it looks.** Perseverance is now caching samples in Jezero's delta, and — exactly as L11 argued — **sample return is realistically the only way to settle it**. Either answer would be profound: a second, independent origin would suggest life is easy and the galaxy full of it; a confirmed absence, after a billion years of water on a well-preserved world, would suggest it is hard. **Mars is the one place where we can realistically get either answer**, which is why it receives the missions.

## Unit II closes: five worlds, one physics

With Mars, Unit II is complete, and its six lectures form a single argument. **L7** built the framework — interiors, the geometric fact that **A/V = 3/R makes size the master variable**, tectonic regimes, cratering as a clock, atmospheric retention, and the three dynamo conditions. **L8** applied it to **Mercury**, whose dead cratered surface and kilometre-high contraction scarps followed from size and distance. **L9** used **Venus** to isolate **water**, whose loss ended the carbon cycle and drove a runaway to 92 bar and +510 K. **L10** turned the apparatus on **Earth**, a world *kept* habitable by active machinery and a negative feedback tracking a brightening Sun. **L11** examined the **Moon** — impact debris, our chronometer, our axial stabiliser. And **L12** closed with **Mars**, the chain from size to death run to completion.

Here is the unit's central claim. Five bodies from the same disk, the same materials, the same laws, diverged into an airless rock, an oven, a living ocean world, a dead companion, and a frozen desert — and almost all of that divergence is explained by **two variables and one contingency**. **Size** sets how long a world stays geologically alive, and hence whether it keeps a dynamo, an atmosphere, and a resupply of volatiles — it explains Mercury, the Moon, and Mars. **Distance** sets the temperature regime and pushed Venus across the runaway threshold. And **water** — whether a world got it and kept it — governs tectonic style, climate-feedback sign, and habitability, which is what separates Earth from Venus. But the honest qualification this course has kept throughout still stands: **not everything reduces to the framework.** Mercury's iron enrichment, the Moon's isotopic identity with Earth, whether Venus was habitable first — these are matters of individual **biography**, and several remain unsolved. **A planet is a general physics problem plus a specific history**, and the framework's value lies as much in isolating what it cannot explain as in what it can. **Next: Unit III, and the giants.** We cross the snow line at last — leaving the small dry worlds behind for Jupiter and Saturn, bodies of hydrogen and helium captured whole from the nebula, with no surfaces, interiors of metallic hydrogen, and a combined mass exceeding everything else orbiting the Sun.$astroMars_master$,
